  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts, but I couldn't run it or fully typecheck it. `@aura3d/engine` isn't installed in this sandbox (`node_modules/@aura3d` is empty). `typecheck` reported only that the module can't be found, plus an implicit-any on the frame callback parameter that follows from it. I wrote the engine calls from the docs and cheat sheet, so some options may not match the real types.\n\n- **Floor and backdrop:** a custom-geometry cove, so the light-grey floor curves up into a seamless wall.\n- **Glass sphere (left):** `material.physical` with transmission 1, ior 1.5, low roughness, clearcoat and opacity 0.35 as a tint.\n- **Metal sphere (centre):** `material.physical` with metallic 1, roughness 0.38 and anisotropy 0.85 for a stretched reflection.\n- **Fabric sphere (right):** a custom GLSL material in dark red. It has a checker weave, fine fuzz noise, wrapped diffuse lighting and a velvet rim sheen. It has no specular reflection.\n- **Lighting:** a studio environment, a hemisphere light, a shadow-casting key, a cool fill, a rim light and contact shadows. The key drifts side to side as a sine wave of x, and the fabric shader's light follows it through a uniform.\n- **Camera and flags:** fixed raised three-quarter-front camera with all three spheres in frame. `window.__TASK_STATE__` is updated every frame and `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after the first frame.\n\nThe glass and metal material fields (transmission, ior, thickness, anisotropy) go through a loose cast because I couldn't confirm they exist in the engine's types. If the engine ignores them, the glass falls back to a plain tint and the metal to a blurred reflection. Run `typecheck` and `vite build` where the package is installed.\n\n",
